    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nConsider the vector [MATH: \\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,3 \\rangle,] shown below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/3309f41e2b21210b444cb4192ea7233c]\n\nContent:\nTo compute the magnitude (length) of [MATH: \\mathbf{a},] we can use the Pythagorean theorem in three dimensions:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: a|= sqrt((2)^{2} + (\u2212 1)^{2} + (3)^{2}); = sqrt(4 + 1 + 9); = sqrt(14)]\n\nContent:\nIn general, given a vector\n[MATH: [a_{x}; a_{y}; a_{z}]]\n\nContent:\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n[MATH: |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2+a_z^2}.]\n\nContent:\nNote\n\nContent:\nWe sometimes use the notation [MATH: || \\mathbf a ||] to denote the magnitude of a vector.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form\n\nExample Prompt:\nLet [MATH: \\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2, -3, -7 \\rangle]. Find [MATH: |\\mathbf{a}|].\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the formula, we have\n[MATH: |a||= sqrt(a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}); = sqrt(2^{2} + (\u2212 3)^{2} + (\u2212 7)^{2}); = sqrt(4 + 9 + 49); = sqrt(62).]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nLet [MATH: a = \u27e87, \u2212 6, 1\u27e9.] Find [MATH: |a|.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(14)]\nb. [MATH: sqrt(86)]\nc. [MATH: 5sqrt(2)]\nd. [MATH: 14]\ne. [MATH: 2sqrt(13)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: b = \u27e83, 3, 3\u27e9], find [MATH: |b|.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 9]\nb. [MATH: 3sqrt(3)]\nc. [MATH: 27]\nd. [MATH: 1]\ne. [MATH: 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector\n\nExample Prompt:\nLet [MATH: [4; \u2212 5; 1]] Find a unit vector that is parallel to [MATH: \\mathbf{a}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nRemember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n[MATH: \\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|}]\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n[MATH: |a||= sqrt(a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}); = sqrt(4^{2} + (\u2212 5)^{2} + 1^{2}); = sqrt(16 + 25 + 1); = sqrt(42).]\n\nFinally, we divide [MATH: \\mathbf{a}] by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n[MATH: u|= (a)/(|a|); = (1)/(sqrt(42))a; = (1)/(sqrt(42)) \u22c5 [4; \u2212 5; 1]; = [4/sqrt(42); \u2212 5/sqrt(42); 1/sqrt(42)]]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nLet [MATH: a = \u27e8 \u2212 2, 8, 3\u27e9.] Find a unit vector that is parallel to [MATH: a.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u27e8(2)/(sqrt(77)), (8)/(sqrt(77)), (3)/(sqrt(77))\u27e9]\nb. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 (2)/(sqrt(77)), \u2212 (8)/(sqrt(77)), \u2212 (3)/(sqrt(77))\u27e9]\nc. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 (2)/(sqrt(77)), (8)/(sqrt(77)), (3)/(sqrt(77))\u27e9]\nd. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 (2)/(sqrt(74)), (8)/(sqrt(74)), (3)/(sqrt(74))\u27e9]\ne. [MATH: \u27e8(2)/(sqrt(74)), (8)/(sqrt(74)), (3)/(sqrt(74))\u27e9]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: p = \u27e8 \u2212 1, 0, 0\u27e9], find a unit vector that is parallel to [MATH: p.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 (1)/(sqrt(2)), (1)/(sqrt(2)), (1)/(sqrt(2))\u27e9]\nb. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 1, 0, 0\u27e9]\nc. [MATH: \u27e80, 1, 0\u27e9]\nd. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 (1)/(sqrt(2)), 0, 0\u27e9]\ne. [MATH: \u27e8 \u2212 (1)/(sqrt(3)), 0, 0\u27e9]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given the Coordinates of its Endpoints\n\nExample Prompt:\nLet [MATH: A(1,1,2)] and [MATH: B(2,-2,3).] Find [MATH: |\\overrightarrow{AB}|.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe have\n\n[MATH: OA^(\u2192) = a = i + j + 2k,; OB^(\u2192) = b = 2i \u2212 2j + 3k.]\n\nExpressing [MATH: \\overrightarrow{AB}] as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n[MATH: AB^(\u2192)|= OB^(\u2192) \u2212 OA^(\u2192); = b \u2212 a; = (2i \u2212 2j + 3k) \u2212 (i + j + 2k); = i \u2212 3j + k.]\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n[MATH: |AB^(\u2192)||= sqrt(1^{2} + (\u2212 3)^{2} + 1^{2}); = sqrt(1 + 9 + 1); = sqrt(11).]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nLet [MATH: A(8, \u2212 2, 3)] and [MATH: B(1, \u2212 3, 0).] Find [MATH: |AB^(\u2192)|.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(51)]\nb. [MATH: 7sqrt(2)]\nc. [MATH: 2sqrt(7)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(39)]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(59)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nLet [MATH: A(2, \u2212 7, 3)] and [MATH: B(5, \u2212 1, 0).] Find [MATH: |AB^(\u2192)|.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(51)]\nb. [MATH: 3sqrt(6)]\nc. [MATH: 2sqrt(6)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(50)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 3sqrt(6)]"
  }
